      "Expected Return and CAPM",
      "The capital asset pricing model (CAPM) agrees with MPT that more risk should bring more potential return. It adds a way to figure a security's expected return:\nexpected return = risk-free rate + beta × (market return − risk-free rate)\n\nThe expected return is a risk-free return, usually a Treasury yield, plus a risk premium. Beta measures the risk. The market's beta is 1.0.\n\nIf a question gives only the market return and beta, treat the risk-free rate as 0%. Then the expected return is simply beta × market return. If the S&P 500 returns 10%, a portfolio with a beta of 1.2 has an expected return of 1.2 × 10% = 12%.\n\nAlpha compares the actual return with that expected return. A positive alpha means the portfolio beat expectations. A negative alpha means it fell short. Say the S&P 500 returned 10%, and a portfolio with a beta of 1.2 actually returned 13%. Its expected return was 12%, so its alpha is 13% − 12% = +1.\n\nThe chart shows CAPM at work. The line is the expected return for each beta. Where a fund lands above or below the line is its alpha.",
