      "s65-c3-emh",
      "The Efficient Market Hypothesis",
      "MPT and CAPM assume markets are efficient and investors are rational. The efficient market hypothesis says the market already has all the information it needs, so every security is priced at what it is worth at any moment. That means investors and advisers cannot beat the market without taking more risk. At best, they can match the market's return minus their costs.\n\nThe theory comes in three forms:\n• Strong form: All public and private information is already in the price. Active management will not pay off. Investors are better off in an index fund or ETF.\n• Semi-strong form: All public information is in the price, but private information is not. The only way to beat the market over time is with inside information, which is illegal to trade on. Retail investors are better off in an index fund or ETF.\n• Weak form: Price charts (technical analysis) cannot predict the market, but studying a company's finances (fundamental analysis) might. This is the form that best supports active management.",
