      "An asset class is a broad group of investments, like stocks, bonds, real estate, or commodities. A sub-class is a type of security within a class.\n\nStrategic asset allocation. The adviser and client set an ideal mix of asset classes by percent. For example: 60% stocks, 20% government bonds, 10% corporate bonds, and 10% cash.\n\nSix months later, different returns may have shifted the mix to 63% stocks, 18% government bonds, 7% corporate bonds, and 12% cash. To fix it, the adviser rebalances, bringing each class back to its target. That can mean using extra cash to buy what is underweight or selling what is overweight.\n\nStrategic allocation fits a buy-and-hold approach, since most assets are held long term and sold only when rebalancing. Fewer trades mean lower trading costs and lower capital gains taxes.\n\nTactical asset allocation tries to time the market. The manager moves in and out of asset classes and sectors based on market signals from fundamental or technical analysis. The plan usually sets a minimum and maximum for each class, and the manager shifts within those limits when conditions favor one type.",
